# UI and UX

- Calm developer-tool look: neutral surfaces, one accent (`primary`), status
  colours only for status (`success`, `warning`, `danger`).
- Colours come from the tokens in `web/src/index.css` (`bg-card`,
  `text-muted-foreground`, `border`...). No raw hex or Tailwind palette colours
  in components. Both light and dark must read well; check both.
- Components follow shadcn/ui patterns: `cva` variants, `cn()` for classes,
  files in `web/src/components/ui/`.
- Every icon-only button has an accessible name (`sr-only` text or
  `aria-label`). Status icons have labels. Errors use `role="alert"`.
- Long jobs show progress steps and keep the output one click away. Never a
  blocking modal during a run.
- Destructive or risky actions ask inline before running, with the fix's
  `confirm` text.
- Layouts work from 375 px wide: stack on small screens, no horizontal scroll.
  A grid that holds cards gets `grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)]` (before
  `lg:grid-cols-2`), so a long name in a card is cut with an ellipsis instead
  of pushing the card past its column. Beside a side menu, columns follow the
  room the content has (`@3xl:grid-cols-2`), not the window. Cards side by
  side are as tall as each other: pair cards of a kind and a size, and a card
  left alone takes the whole row. A row of tiles fills the row (`tileSpan`).
- Keyboard first: visible focus, Enter submits, actions reachable by Tab.

## Components: one part for each job

Every screen is made of the parts in `web/src/components/ui/`; the mockup's
**Parts** page (`/mockup/parts`, `web/src/pages/ui-kit.tsx`) shows each one
with its rule. A page never draws its own copy. `web/test/ui-ratchet.test.ts`
counts the hand-made copies left (raw `<button>`, px text sizes, status
tints, `rounded-xl border` cards, `LoaderCircle` spinners, hand-styled links,
Reload words, raw `<dl>`) and fails when a count goes up; a change that
lowers one lowers the baseline there too.

- **Tokens.** Type: `text-2xl` page titles, `text-sm` body, `text-dense`
  (13px) dense rows and code, `text-xs` captions, `text-2xs` (11px) only in
  badges and counts; never a size in px. Corners: `rounded-xl` surfaces (cards,
  dialogs), `rounded-lg` boxes inside a card (tiles, notices), `rounded-md`
  controls, `rounded-full` badges and dots only. Icons `size-4`, `size-3.5`
  in small text and badges, `size-8` in empty states. Gaps 2, 3, 4 or 6.
  `cn()` knows the panel's text sizes (`lib/utils.ts`): add a new size there
  too, or tailwind-merge drops it as a colour.
- **Card**: `Card`, `CardHeader` (`title`, `description`, one `meta` on the
  right: a `ScopeLabel`, a `StatusBadge` or a `CountBadge`, then `actions`; no
  decorative icons), `CardBody` (`flush` for rows and tables, `compact`),
  `CardSection` (a titled part inside), `CardFooter` (buttons on the left, the
  way out in `end`: a `TextLink` with `arrow`). A job is the card's footer.
- **Buttons**: one primary (solid) per page header or card, then `outline`,
  then `soft` (a light fill) for the rest. `ghost` only for icon buttons and
  toolbars: a worded button with no fill does not read as a button.
  `danger` only inside a confirm. An icon-only button is `IconButton`, whose
  label is required. Sizes `default`, `sm`, `xs`, `icon`, `icon-sm`.
- **Links**: `TextLink` (`primary` in text, `muted` beside facts, `plain` for
  a name that opens its page, `arrow` for a way out of a card). An http link
  opens a new tab with its arrow-out icon. A link goes somewhere, a button
  does something.
- **Status**: `Badge` labels a thing, `StatusBadge` says how it stands (ok,
  warn, fail, off, running; the same colour for the same state everywhere),
  `CountBadge` is a number in a tab, menu or filter, `StatusDot` a site's
  health. `Notice` is the only tinted box, one tone per box (danger, warning,
  success, info), `size="inline"` inside a card. `Spinner` is the only
  turning icon.
- **Facts and rows**: `KeyValue` (`inline` with a label column, or `stacked`),
  `StatTile` in `StatTiles` for a row of numbers that open the rest,
  `RowList` and `ListRow` for rows in a card (start, name and a line under it,
  badges, actions and a `Menu`).
- **Navigation**: `Breadcrumbs` above every page below the menu's top level
  (on a phone only the way back shows); `Tabs` for a page's views,
  `Tabs size="sm"` for a tab's own views (level 2); a detail page's sections
  in `SectionLayout` (a menu down the side from `lg` in a frame like the
  cards, each section with its icon as the main menu has, tabs below it), with
  the open section's own views listed under it (`sectionViews` in
  `lib/site-tabs.ts`); below `lg` the section draws them as small tabs.
  `Segmented` only switches how the same thing shows (Compact, Details).
- **Lists**: `FilterBar` over a list (search, then `FilterChips` with counts,
  then pickers and the view switch at its end).
- **Forms**: `Field` (label over the control, hint under it), `SettingRow`
  (title and hint left, the control right) for settings, `Checkbox`,
  `Switch`, `Select`, `Input`, `SecretInput`, and `SaveBar` for a form with
  several fields.

## One pattern on every page

- **A job shows where it was started**: as the footer of the card whose
  button started it (`JobFooter`, or `JobBox` under a `border-t` line, in
  `web/src/features/jobs/job-box.tsx`), scrolled into view, with its steps,
  Retry when it failed and the link to Undo once it settled. Never a box or
  card of its own between cards: the cards around it stay where they are. A
  job started from a row's menu in a long list shows at the top of that list,
  inside its card. The header's jobs indicator is the view of every job; a
  page never adds a second place for the same job.
- **Changes you can undo is the last card of its view**, full width, and only
  there while something can be undone (`CleanupsCard`, `RowChangesCard`): at
  the bottom it never pushes another card when it appears. The toast after a
  change has its own Undo as well.
- **A form with several fields saves through `SaveBar`**: it sticks to the
  bottom of the window while there are changes ("2 unsaved changes",
  Discard, and the one button that saves or applies, named for what it does:
  "Save and restart Apache", "Apply"). A single switch or button acts at once.
- **Every card that changes something says what it applies to** with
  `ScopeLabel` in its header: "all sites", "PHP 8.3", "this site".
- **An inherited value always shows its number and where it comes from**:
  "Inherit: 128M (php.ini)", "Inherit: 2M (PHP's built-in default)". Never a
  bare "default".
- **Global things sit above per-item things.** A page about many items (PHP
  versions, databases) starts with what applies to all of them, then the item
  you picked, under its own heading.
- **Tabs come in groups and keep their place.** Related tabs sit together
  (a site: Overview and Logs; WordPress and its data; the server) with a line
  between groups, and a tab an item lacks leaves the rest where they are. A
  tab's badge counts only what needs a look, colored by how bad it is, never
  how many things the tab lists. An Overview shows a fact from each other tab
  and opens it; a setting lives in its tab, next to what it changes.
- **One word for one thing.** A site with a PHP version or settings of its
  own has its "own PHP"; the version every other site runs is "the default".
- **Confirm dialogs**: the title asks the question, the description says
  what happens and what Undo does, Cancel on the left, the action named for
  what it does on the right.
- **One card per job, with its choices inside.** Ways to do the same thing
  are choices in one card, not cards of their own: a database comes in
  through **Import a database** (production's saved server, or a dump from
  any source), and whether it is cleaned is **The copy** (`CopyChoice`, As it
  is or Cleaned), the same control in the new site wizard.
- **Dialogs with long content** pass their buttons as `footer`, so the
  content scrolls between the title and them; a row's dialogs also pass
  `fullScreen` (`useRowDialogFull`), remembered as a preference. Confirm
  dialogs stay small.
- **Dialog widths come from `size`, never a class**: `sm` (24rem) a short
  question, `md` (32rem, the default) a confirm or a few fields, `lg` (48rem)
  choices side by side, lists and previews (the web server switch, .htaccess
  rules, export), `xl` (64rem) tables, rows and diffs (the row editor, a
  result row), and `fullScreen` on top. `web/test/dialog-sizes.test.ts` fails
  on a width passed in `className`.
- **The menu is three groups** (Work, Server, System, in
  `components/layout/app-shell.tsx`), every page visible, each with a short hint or
  a live count on the right. Group by what a page is for, not by technology:
  a site's Performance tab holds its profiles and slow queries together.
- **A page that moves keeps its old address**: add it to `lib/moved.ts`,
  which sends it on with its query, and test it in `web/test/moved.test.ts`.
- **A long tool page starts with what needs a look** (`NeedsALook`): one link
  per card that found something, worst first, scrolling to that card, and
  nothing when all is fine. Its numbers count what the card counts as it
  opens (`cleanupRows` for the clean-up card), so the two never disagree.
- **A link to a card on the same page goes through `scrollToCard`**
  (`lib/scroll-to.ts`): smooth scroll (at once with reduced motion), a ring
  on the card for two seconds, and focus on it. Never a bare
  `scrollIntoView` for a link: without the ring you cannot tell which card
  you landed on.
- **Secrets use `SecretInput`, never a password field.** A sudo password, an
  API key, a site's or a database's password is text drawn as dots with every
  password manager's ignore marker (`web/src/lib/secret-field.ts`), so the
  browser never offers to save it. Only the panel login and the panel
  password use `PasswordInput`; `web/test/secret-field.test.ts` keeps it so.

## Long cards

A card should fit the window (900px high is the check: `npm run ui:inventory`
opens every page at 1440x900). Three ways, never a scroll box inside a card
(code, logs and diffs excepted):

- **A long list** shows its first rows and Show more: 21 light rows, 10 heavy
  ones (a query with its SQL), see below.
- **Several things of one kind** (a site's repos in the Git card) show one at
  a time behind `Tabs size="sm"`.
- **A long form** folds its groups with `Fold`: the first group and every
  group with a value set here open, the rest folded with a note of what is
  inside ("5 settings", "2 set here"). Folded fields stay in the page, so what
  you typed is kept.

## Long lists in cards

A list inside a summary card (the WordPress view's options, meta keys, tables,
issues, users, clean-up kinds) shows its first `WP_DB_LIST_ROWS` (7) rows and
a **Show all N** link under it, remembered per site and list in this
browser (`dmp-wpdb-show-all` in localStorage), so another site opens short. When the rows it hides can still be acted on (ticked
clean-up kinds), the link says how many. A name or a count that has rows
behind it opens them in Tables: plain filters or a clean-up's kind go in the
history entry (`navigate(..., { state })`), never the address.

A long list that is a card of its own (a database's tables, slow queries, a
site's scheduled events) shows its first `LIST_STEP` (21) rows with
`useShowMore` and `ShowMore` from `components/ui/show-more.tsx`: "21 of 60",
**Show 21 more**, **Show all 60**, **Show fewer**. A search or a filter
starts it short again (`reset`). Use it for a ranked list the browser already
has; a list the server pages (a table's rows) keeps its pages.

## One layout per page type

Every page is made of the pieces in `web/src/components/layout/page.tsx` and
`web/src/components/layout/skeletons.tsx`, and follows the layout of its type.

- **Width by page type** (`lib/page-width.ts`): the top bar runs the whole
  width (search left, what changes while you work right). Lists and grids of
  like items (Sites, Services, Activity, WP Cores) run up to 120rem and add
  columns (three from 96rem of content, four from 110rem); detail pages (a
  site, a job, a run, a section of Services) stop at 100rem and keep their
  cards in pairs; settings and flows (Settings, Update, the new site wizard)
  stop at 64rem. Never the whole screen for text or forms.
- **The pieces.** `PageHeader` (title and intro left, actions right, Check
  again last) tops a page; `ViewBar` (a picker or filters left, Check again
  right, "Read 2 min ago" and a short note under it, or in the picker's place
  when there is none, so the bar is one line) tops a tab or a view.
  The button that reads a page again is always `CheckAgain`: never Refresh or
  Read again. Something the whole page or view should know is a `Notice` right
  under its header or bar (danger, warning, info); a field's own error stays
  next to the field. Nothing yet is an `EmptyState`: what would be here and
  the way to get it.
- **Loading shows the page's shape**, never a blank box or a "Reading..." line
  alone: a skeleton of the real layout with a `Reading` line that says what is
  read (`CardsSkeleton`, `ListSkeleton`, `TableSkeleton`, `LogSkeleton`, and
  `ReadingBlock` inside a card). A part read after the page (a tile, a card)
  keeps its place and fills in.
- **List pages** (Sites and its Removed filter, Activity's Jobs and Run history, WP Cores, Mail,
  Aura): header, notices, a filter bar, then one card of rows. Loading is a
  list or table skeleton; nothing is an empty state.
- **Detail pages** (a site, a job, a run): breadcrumbs, title and facts,
  one primary action and the rest in a menu, then its sections, Overview
  first: a site's run down its side (`SectionLayout`), a tab's own views as
  small tabs. The page's whole width: their steps,
  output and tables need it. A narrow column (`max-w-3xl`) is for forms and
  reading: the new-site wizard, Updates and its release notes.
- **Status pages** (Aura's Machine checks, Developer options, Updates, Services and its
  Apache, nginx and Cron tabs): header with Check again, notices, the summary
  first, then **pairs then full width**: short summary cards side by side,
  long lists and logs across the whole width at the bottom.
- **Tool pages** (a site's Database: WordPress, Backups and tools; Services,
  MySQL): a `ViewBar`, then **two independent columns** of cards, each card one
  task with its job as its footer, and Changes you can undo last at full
  width.
- **Settings pages** (a site's PHP and Config, Services, PHP per version, cron
  tasks): one column of cards, each with its `ScopeLabel`, a `SaveBar` while
  there are changes, a job in the card it changes.
- **Data pages** (Tables, a table's rows and structure, SQL): a bar, one large
  card with the table and its paging, then Undo last.
- **Flows** (New site and its steps, login, an update's install) keep their
  own step layout.
